course: dictionary
lesson: 12812
title: Longer and longer
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Elongation is a noun, and its verb is elongate. To elongate something is to make it longer, and an elongated thing is long and thin. The word has long inside it, which is a clue that it is about length.
explain: «The plant showed elongation as its stem grew up toward the window.» The stem got longer as it grew, so elongation means growing longer. Swap in "making longer" and the sentence still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: «The scientist measured the elongation of the rubber band before it broke.» Here elongation means how far the band stretched before it broke. Look for clues like stretch, grow, long or thin, and you will often find the meaning.
fact: Elongation means making something longer, or becoming longer.
fact: «Elongation» is a noun, «elongate» is the verb, and «elongated» describes something long and thin.
fact: In science, elongation is how much a material stretches before it breaks.
fact: «Stem elongation» means a plant's stem growing longer, often toward the light.
fact: Springs and elastic bands show elongation when you pull them.
exercise: «The stem showed elongation as it grew toward the sunny window.» What does elongation mean here? | Painting the stem green | Cutting the stem into pieces | Making the stem shorter | *Making the stem longer by growing
exercise: Maya says an elongated shape is a round, fat one. What does elongated really mean? | Bright and shiny | *Long and thin | Very small | Round and fat
exercise: Which word means to make something longer? | Elegant | Elope | *Elongate | Elbow
test: «The spring's elongation was measured before it broke.» What does elongation mean in this science sentence? | *How far the spring stretched before it broke | How heavy the spring was | How quickly the spring rusted | The colour of the spring
test: Which of these is an elongated shape? | A round ball | *A long, thin pencil | A tiny dot | A flat square tile
exam: Why does a plant's stem show elongation near a window? | To shrink in the cold | To change colour in the dark | *To grow longer toward the light | To fall off the plant
